Could A Divorced Parent Designate A Custodian for their Children By Will in China?
Recently, Mr. Y consults our team about such dispute between he and his ex-wife. Based on the case, here we conduct legal analysis on whether a divorced parent could designate a custodian by will in China.
The legal ground is Article 29 of the Civil Code of People’s Republic of China (hereinafter as Civil Code), “where the parents of a ward are his or her guardians, they may designate the guardian by a will”. Since L is not only the son’s parent but also his current physical custodian, the designation of custodian by will is valid and legal.
a) The parents of a minor child are his or her statutory custodians.
iii. Other individuals or organizations willing to act as the custodian, provided that it is approved by the urban residents' committee, villagers' committee, or civil affairs department of the place of the minor's domicile.
The designation of custodian by will should be based on consensus of the parents despite that the parents are divorced. As long as one of the parents is alive, the physical custody will be automatically granted to the living parent unless legally deprived of.
It is advisable that the parent confirm with the designated person in advance and reach an agreement on such designation if possible. In practice, there were cases that the designated person was not willing to act as custodian.
The General Provisions of the Civil Law of the People's Republic of China expired and be replaced by Civil Code on Jan 1, 2021.
Research / Articles Right of Residence and Cross-border Estate Planning for Properties in China
First, right of residence is a right in real properties, and to be more speciﬁc, a right created in residential properties only.
Article 366 An owner of right of residence is, pursuant to the terms of contract, entitled to a usufructuary right of possessing and using the residential properties of others for purpose of satisfying the need of residence in life.
(1) it is a property right, a usufructuary right, not purely a contractual or obligatory right, even though it is created out of a contract. It entitles the holder of the right to possess and use a physical property, in this case, a home; it is worthy of a note that this right of residence can only be created upon residential properties, not commercial properties.
(3) it is a right created by law to satisfy the basic life need of residence, not for proﬁts, not for business purpose. In practice, it is most likely to be created for family members or someone that has a close personal relationship to the owner of the property such as a personal caretaker for elderly people.
Article 367 of China Civil Code provides for the way to set up a right of residence:
Pursuant to Article 368, a right of residence shall be created gratuitously, connoting that this right is applicable most likely in family setting. However this same Article has proviso allowing the parties to the right of residence to be created for a fee.
As you may know, China Trust Law oﬀers little help in family estate planning esp when it comes to assets like real estates. This new legal regime is no perfect alternative to a fully functional family trust, it does solve some practical problems that otherwise are not solvable before.
Unfortunately, this doesn’t work in China. With the right of residence as carved by China Civil Code, now the client can provide in his will that the property is devised to his children on the condition that the children set up the right of residence in favor of his surviving wife.
It shall be well noted that China Civil Code has not yet come into eﬀect till beginning of the next year of 2021.
