On June 29, 2020, China oﬃcially published its long-awaited regulation on cosmetic industry, titled as “Regulation on Supervision and Administration of Cosmetics” (hereinafter “New Regulation“).
This post focuses on those rules in the New Regulation in regard of the administration and use of new cosmetic materials in end products.
Chinese Version of Article 9 of the China Regulation on Supervision and Administration of Cosmetics
This proclaiming provision sets the tone for industrial attitude for new technologies and innovations.
Under the New Regulation, raw materials/ingredients are classiﬁed into two classes: new raw materials and used raw materials. Used materials are put on a list maintained by China National Medical Products Administration (“NMPA“, formerly known as “CFDA“).
(3) other data concerning manufacturing techniques, stability and quality control standards; and
How long will an approval be issued after submitting all data?
The major breakthrough in the New Regulation that is widely welcomed is the imposition of a time limit for NMPA to register a high-hazard new ingredient. Article 13 reads:
In the meantime, it shall be cautioned that possible delay could be anticipated if the Technical Evaluation Institution or NMPA requires the applicants to supplement technical data during the process.
