News - RPT-COLUMN-China will have to do more to cool the surge in iron ore: Russell
RPT-COLUMN-China will have to do more to cool the surge in iron ore: Russell
Company News Reuters, Luxton, Australia, May 13-If China wants to ease the hot iron ore and steel industries, it may do more than talk and repair profits.
The Dalian Commodity Exchange (DCE) increased the trading limit and margin for iron ore contracts or deliveries from January to April 2022 in June, September, October and December and from the trading day on May 11 Claim.
If the purpose is to cool the market’s enthusiasm for the soaring iron ore and steel prices this year, it is useless. DCE iron ore prices closed at a new closing high of 1,337 yuan (US$207) per ton on Wednesday.
Coronavirus outbreak. The commodity price reporting agency Argus assessed that the spot iron ore MT-IO-QIN62 = ARG shipped to North China also climbed to a record high on Wednesday, closing at US$235.55 per ton.
Obviously, at least so far, the promise of reducing pollution in the steel industry has not led to a decline in production.
Another factor within China’s control is the ongoing dispute with Australia. Australia is the world’s largest iron ore miner and a supplier of 70% of China’s imports.
Considering China’s heavy dependence on Australian iron ore, this seems unlikely, but the problem may have gone from everywhere, at least to the peripheral vision of the market.
And operations. According to data compiled by Refinitiv, Australia’s exports in April were 71.35 million tons, down from 76.67 million tons in March and 75.62 million tons in April 2020.
However, this export level of Brazil is still far below the 35 million tons per month reached in August and September last year, indicating that there is still a lot of room for growth in the shipments of South American producers.
The stocks of iron ore SH-TOT-IRONINV at Chinese ports are not particularly high either. It was 13105 million tons in the week ending May 7, down from 133.1 million tons in the previous week.
However, Beijing may also receive some clear signals that steel production will be restricted this year and iron ore inventories will rebound to convince the market that the rally may be over. (Edited by Michael Perry)
