PWM low-frequency dimming becomes the original sin
However, being more “ideal” also means paying more. The “eye-wounding theory” of AMOLED displays is more from the outside world’s dissatisfaction with the current AMOLED manufacturers generally adopting PWM low-frequency dimming.
Since LCD screens rely on LED backlight panels to emit light, in the field of smart phones, LCD screens mostly use DC dimming. This is a technology that adjusts the brightness by directly controlling the current on both sides of the light-emitting component.
DC dimming does not seem to be suitable for AMOLED screens. AMOLED screen is a technology that relies on organic materials to emit light.
In fact, this problem has not been solved well even now. Perhaps it is just that, PWM dimming has become another option and has entered everyone’s attention.
The current Samsung AMOLED screens all use 250Hz low-frequency PWM dimming technology. When the screen brightness is lower, the possibility that the human eye can perceive it becomes greater, which is more likely to affect sensitive people.
