Jamaica is short of cannabis. Heavy rainfall followed by a prolonged drought, an increase in local consumption and a decline in the number of marijuana farmers have caused Jamaica's weed shortage in the island's famous but largely illegal market, which experts say is the worst they've ever seen.
Long associated with cannabis, reggae and Rastafarians, Jamaica allowed a regulated medical marijuana industry in 2015 and decriminalized small amounts of weed.
The problem was compounded by the severe COVID-19measures, including a 18.00 p.m. curfew, which meant farmers couldn't maintain their fields at night as is customary, said Kenrick Wallace, 29, who, along with 20 other farmers, work 2 acres.
